  "metadata": {
    "job": "Round bar center finder",
    "revision": "B",
    "notes": "Glue the fence under the plate, flush along the plate's two straight edges, so the lips hang down; pin the parts together through the four ⌀1.5 alignment holes (wire or brads) while the glue sets, then pull the pins. Set the plate on the end of the bar and push the corner against it: the lips' inside faces touch the bar, so its center is on the slot's line. Scribe along the slot, move the tool round the bar, scribe again; the lines cross at the center. barMax and barMin set the largest and smallest bar (50 and 10mm); the plate, the slot and the fence follow. Keep barMax at 20 or more: below about 16 the tail's length goes negative, which the app ignores, so the plate stops following the variable. Keep lipW at 3 or more so the pin holes stay inside the lip; the hole diameter is a plain dimension, so change it together with the drill. The holes are drilled first, the slot cleared next and the outlines last, because an outline is what frees a part. No tabs: hold the sheet down with double-sided tape. Feeds are starting points for 3mm aluminium on a rigid hobby mill; tune them for your own machine."
  },
